-- title:
--   Hecke degeneracy legs pull the j-finite chart back exactly
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$, an integer $M \ge 5$ with $p \nmid M$, and a field $L$ of characteristic zero which is a $\{p\}$-cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$, together with a primitive $p$-th root of unity $\zeta \in L$. Let $K$ be the intermediate field of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ obtained as the $L$-subfield generated by the image, under coefficientwise extension of scalars $\mathbb{Q} \to L$, of the function field $\mathrm{x1FunctionField}(Mp)$ (the $\mathbb{Q}$-subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated by the $q$-expansion ratios attached to $\Gamma_1(Mp)$). Let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$ such that $p$ lies in its maximal ideal and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$, and let $j \in K$ be an element whose underlying Laurent series is the coefficientwise image of $\mathrm{jq} = q^{-1}\cdot(\text{integral } j\text{-numerator})$, assumed nonzero. Fix a prime $\ell$ and let $K_\ell$ be the analogous base change to $L$ of the function field attached to $\Gamma_1(Mp) \cap \Gamma_0(Mp\ell)$, with an element $j_\ell \in K_\ell$, nonzero, again with Laurent series $\mathrm{jq}$; both $K$ and $K_\ell$ carry compatible $A$-algebra structures over $L$. Assume $\mathrm{HeckeBetaOneDefined}\,(Mp)\,\ell$: every $y$ in the $\mathbb{Q}$-function field of $\Gamma_1(Mp)$ has $q \mapsto q^\ell$ substitute $\mathrm{qExpand}\,\mathbb{Q}\,\ell\,y$ lying in the function field of $\Gamma_1(Mp) \cap \Gamma_0(Mp\ell)$. Let $\pi_\alpha, \pi_\beta$ be morphisms over $\operatorname{Spec} A$ from the two-chart model of $(A, K_\ell, j_\ell)$ (the pushout of the spectra of the two chart algebras, the algebra of elements integral over $A[j_\ell]$ and over $A[j_\ell^{-1}]$) to the two-chart model of $(A, K, j)$, each finite and surjective on points. Let $\iota_\alpha, \iota_\beta$ be $A$-algebra maps from the $j$-finite chart algebra of $(A,K,j)$ to that of $(A,K_\ell,j_\ell)$ which on underlying Laurent series act as the identity, respectively as $\mathrm{qExpand}\,L\,\ell$, and assume that on the $j$-finite chart the morphism $\iota_{\mathrm{Fin}}$ followed by $\pi_\alpha$ (respectively $\pi_\beta$) equals $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\iota_\alpha$ (respectively $\iota_\beta$) followed by $\iota_{\mathrm{Fin}}$ of the target. The conclusion is the conjunction of two equalities of open subsets: the preimage under $\pi_\alpha$, and likewise under $\pi_\beta$, of the open range of $\iota_{\mathrm{Fin}}$ for $(A,K,j)$ equals the open range of $\iota_{\mathrm{Fin}}$ for $(A,K_\ell,j_\ell)$.
--
--   This is the chart-compatibility statement for the two degeneracy maps relating the integral two-chart models of $X_1(Mp)$ and of the Hecke correspondence level $\Gamma_1(Mp) \cap \Gamma_0(Mp\ell)$: each leg carries the $j$-finite chart of the roof exactly onto the preimage of the $j$-finite chart of the base. It is formulated for consumers which have only finiteness, surjectivity and the two finite-chart squares available, and it feeds the analysis of the degeneracy maps on components of the special fibre.
